⟦P0⟧Tutorial: Find and fix vulnerable dependencies with ⟦P1⟧ Code Go⟦P2⟧⟦P3⟧Back to Go Security⟦P4⟧⟦P5⟧You can scan your code for vulnerabilities directly out of your editor with the Go extension for Visual Studio Code.⟦P6⟧Note: for an explanation of the vulnerability fix included in the images below, see the ⟦P7⟧govulncheck tutorial⟦P8⟧.⟦P9⟧Prerequisites:⟦P10⟧⟦P11⟧Go.⟦P12⟧ We recommend using the latest version of Go to follow this tutorial. For installation instructions, see ⟦P13⟧Installing Go⟦P14⟧.⟦P15⟧⟦P16⟧⟦P17⟧ Code⟦P18⟧, updated to the latest version. ⟦P19⟧Download here⟦P20⟧. You can also use Vim (see ⟦P21⟧here⟦P22⟧ for details), but this tutorial focuses on ⟦P23⟧ Code Go.⟦P24⟧⟦P25⟧⟦P26⟧ Code Go extension⟦P27⟧, which can be ⟦P28⟧downloaded here⟦P29⟧.⟦P30⟧⟦P31⟧Editor-specific settings changes.⟦P32⟧ You will need to modify your ⟦P33⟧ settings according to ⟦P34⟧these specifications⟦P35⟧ before being able to replicate the results below.⟦P36⟧How to scan for vulnerabilities using ⟦P37⟧ Code Go⟦P38⟧⟦P39⟧Step 1.⟦P40⟧ Run "Go: Toggle Vulncheck"⟦P41⟧The ⟦P42⟧Toggle Vulncheck⟦P43⟧ command displays vulnerability analysis for all the dependencies listed in your modules. To use this command, open the ⟦P44⟧command palette⟦P45⟧ in your ⟦P46⟧ (Ctrl+Shift+P on ⟦P47⟧ or Cmd+Shift+P on Mac ⟦P48⟧) and run “Go: Toggle ⟦P49⟧ In your ⟦P50⟧ file, you will see the diagnostics for vulnerable dependencies that are used both directly and indirectly in your code.⟦P51⟧Run Toggle Vulncheck⟦P52⟧Note: To reproduce this tutorial on your own editor, copy the code below into your ⟦P53⟧ file.⟦P54⟧This program takes ⟦P55⟧ tags as command-line⟦P56⟧arguments and parses them.⟦P57⟧Then, make sure the corresponding ⟦P58⟧ file for the program looks like this:⟦P59⟧Now, run ⟦P60⟧ to ensure that your ⟦P61⟧ file is updated.⟦P62⟧⟦P63⟧Step 2.⟦P64⟧ Run govulncheck via a code action.⟦P65⟧Running govulncheck using a code action allows you to focus on the dependencies that are actually called in your code. Code actions in ⟦P66⟧ Code are marked by lightbulb icons; hover over the relevant dependency to see information about the vulnerability, then select “Quick Fix” to be shown a menu of options. Of these, choose “run govulncheck to ⟦P67⟧ This will return the relevant govulncheck output in your terminal.⟦P68⟧govulncheck code action⟦P69⟧⟦P70⟧ Code Go govulncheck output⟦P71⟧⟦P72⟧Step 3⟦P73⟧. Hover over a dependency listed in your ⟦P74⟧ file.⟦P75⟧The relevant govulncheck output about a specific dependency can also be found by hovering over the dependency in the ⟦P76⟧ file. For a quick look at dependency information, this option is even more efficient than using a code action.⟦P77⟧Hover over dependency for vulnerability information⟦P78⟧⟦P79⟧Step 4.⟦P80⟧ Upgrade to a "fixed in" version of your dependency.⟦P81⟧Code actions can also be used to quickly upgrade to a version of your dependency where the vulnerability is fixed. Do this by selecting the “Upgrade” option in the code action drop-down menu.⟦P82⟧Upgrade to Latest via code action menu⟦P83⟧Additional resources⟦P84⟧See ⟦P85⟧this page⟦P86⟧ for more information about vulnerability scanning in your ⟦P87⟧. The ⟦P88⟧Notes and Caveats section⟦P89⟧, in particular, discusses special cases for which vulnerability scanning may be more complex than in the example above.⟦P90⟧The ⟦P91⟧Go Vulnerability Database⟦P92⟧ contains information from many existing sources in addition to direct reports by Go package maintainers to the Go security team.⟦P93⟧See ⟦P94⟧Go Vulnerability Management⟦P95⟧ page provides a high-level view of Go's architecture for detecting, reporting and managing vulnerabilities.⟦P96⟧
